5. Free Uses and the Commercial-Success Threshold

All permitted uses are free of royalties while a Product Family's Product
Revenue for the applicable Product Revenue Year does not exceed US $10,000,000.
This includes commercial and proprietary use. At exactly US $10,000,000, no
royalty is owed and no revenue report is required.

Personal, community, research, educational, and nonprofit uses that generate no
Product Revenue remain free. The revenue of an unrelated product or the general
revenue of a company does not count toward a Product Family's threshold.

6. Progressive Commercial-Success Royalty

When Product Revenue for a Product Family exceeds US $10,000,000 during a
Product Revenue Year, Licensee owes a marginal royalty calculated separately for
that Product Family as follows:

Product Revenue bracket                              Marginal rate
US $0 through US $10,000,000                         0%
Above US $10,000,000 through US $25,000,000          0.5%
Above US $25,000,000 through US $100,000,000         1.0%
Above US $100,000,000                                2.0%

These are progressive brackets. A higher rate applies only to Product Revenue
within that higher bracket and does not apply retroactively to revenue in a
lower bracket.

For clarity, the cumulative royalty for a Product Revenue Year is:

0.5% of Product Revenue above US $10,000,000 and up to US $25,000,000;
plus
1.0% of Product Revenue above US $25,000,000 and up to US $100,000,000;
plus
2.0% of Product Revenue above US $100,000,000.

Example:

Product Revenue: $50,000,000
First $10,000,000:
0% = $0
Next $15,000,000:
0.5% = $75,000
Next $25,000,000:
1.0% = $250,000
Total royalty:
$325,000

Amounts are stated in United States dollars. Revenue denominated in another
currency must be converted using a reasonable, consistently applied, publicly
available exchange rate for the date or reporting period in question.

7. Product Revenue

7.1. Included Revenue. "Product Revenue" means the gross revenue earned,
received, or receivable from commercial exploitation of the Product Family by
Licensee, its Affiliates, or a publisher, distributor, storefront, or other
party acting for or monetizing the Product Family on their behalf, less only the
permitted deductions in Section 7.2. Product Revenue includes revenue
attributable to:

(a) product sales;

(b) subscriptions;

(c) downloadable content and expansions;

(d) in-app purchases;

(e) advertising;

(f) licensing;

(g) marketplace, access, participation, or service fees; and

(h) any other commercial exploitation of the Product Family.

7.2. Permitted Deductions. Product Revenue may be reduced by reasonable,
documented amounts actually incurred for:

(a) sales tax, value-added tax, goods and services tax, and similar transaction
taxes collected from customers and remitted to a government;

(b) refunds and credits actually issued to customers;

(c) chargebacks;

(d) mandatory commissions retained by an unaffiliated storefront or platform
as a condition of distributing or selling the Product Family; and

(e) payment-processing fees charged by an unaffiliated payment processor.

7.3. Non-Deductible Expenses. Product Revenue may not be reduced by ordinary
business expenses, including salaries, contractor costs, development costs,
marketing, hosting, customer support, financing costs, interest, publisher
revenue shares, other royalty obligations, overhead, or general operating
expenses.

7.4. Excluded Amounts. Product Revenue excludes unrelated company revenue,
equity financing, investment proceeds, loans, unrelated grants, unrelated
donations, and revenue from unrelated products.

7.5. Aggregation and Anti-Splitting. Product Revenue from the same Product
Family must be aggregated across Licensee and all Affiliates. Product Revenue
may not be divided or recharacterized among subsidiaries, Affiliates,
publishers, editions, platforms, ports, storefronts, controlled entities,
agreements, or distribution channels to avoid a threshold or reduce a royalty.
Revenue earned before and after a transfer, reorganization, publisher change,
or similar transaction remains part of the same Product Revenue Year when the
commercially continuous Product Family remains substantially the same.

8. Notice, Statements, and Payment

8.1. No Reporting Below the Threshold. If Product Revenue for a Product Family
does not exceed US $10,000,000 in a Product Revenue Year, Licensee has no revenue
reporting or royalty-payment obligation under this License for that Product
Family and Product Revenue Year.

8.2. Threshold Notice. Within thirty days after Product Revenue for a Product
Family first exceeds US $10,000,000 in a Product Revenue Year, Licensee must
notify Licensor at the notice contact identified above. The notice must identify
the Licensee and Product Family and provide a business contact for reporting and
payment.

8.3. Quarterly Statements. After the threshold is exceeded, Licensee must
provide a royalty statement for each remaining reporting quarter in that Product
Revenue Year, including the reporting quarter in which the threshold was
exceeded. Each statement is due within forty-five days after the reporting
quarter ends and must contain enough information to calculate:

(a) gross revenue included in Product Revenue;

(b) each category of permitted deduction;

(c) cumulative Product Revenue for the Product Revenue Year;

(d) the applicable progressive brackets and rates;

(e) the cumulative royalty owed for the Product Revenue Year; and

(f) previous royalty payments credited for that Product Revenue Year.

8.4. Payment. Royalties accrued through the end of a reporting quarter are due
within forty-five days after that quarter ends, less royalty payments already
made for the same Product Revenue Year. Licensee must provide a final
reconciliation statement and any remaining payment within forty-five days after
the Product Revenue Year ends.

9. Records and Audit

After a Product Family exceeds the threshold, Licensee must retain records
reasonably necessary to verify Product Revenue, deductions, statements, and
payments for four years after the applicable statement was due.

No more than once in any twelve-month period for a Product Family, Licensor may,
on reasonable advance written notice, have an independent licensed accountant
or comparably qualified professional inspect those relevant records. The audit
must occur during normal business hours, avoid unreasonable disruption, and be
limited to compliance with this License.

The auditor and Licensor must protect confidential commercial information and
may use or disclose it only as necessary to verify and enforce this License or
as required by law. Licensor normally pays the audit costs. If an audit finds
that Licensee underpaid royalties for the audited period by more than five
percent, Licensee must promptly pay the shortfall and reimburse Licensor for the
reasonable audit costs.

10. Alternative Commercial Agreements

Licensee and Licensor may enter into a separate written commercial agreement.
Possible terms may include fixed annual fees, royalty caps, lower negotiated
rates, enterprise licensing, simplified reporting, or other custom terms. A
separately signed agreement supersedes the royalty, reporting, or other
provisions of this License only to the extent that agreement expressly says it
does.

